<title>USA: U.S. Feb producer prices fell 0.4 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Feb producer prices fell 0.4 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				 Feb    Jan	 Feb97/96</p>
<p> Finished Goods	     -0.4   -0.3		2.2</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 -0.1   Unch		0.5</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	    -0.3   -1.0		2.4</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		-1.2   -0.2		9.8</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  132.2  132.6		n/a</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods		Feb    Jan	 Feb97/96</p>
<p> -Gasoline		    -3.1    2.6	     20.4</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		 -1.1   -2.8	     18.5</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	  -0.2    0.1		2.6</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	     0.2   -0.4		0.4</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	 -0.1   Unch		0.3</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	 -0.1    0.2		1.2</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 Unch    0.1	     -0.1</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	-0.1    0.1	     -0.4</p>
<p> -Construction Materials   0.4    0.1		2.6</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  Feb    Jan	 Feb97/96</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods   -0.6    1.1	     10.0</p>
<p> Crude Goods		  -5.9    5.2		6.3</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X	1.0    2.0	     -2.5</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	   -1.9   -1.0	     -3.7</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	 -8.3    9.3	     13.6</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y     -12.4   12.9	     24.2</p>
<p> -Petroleum		  -12.2    4.1	     23.1</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast February producer prices to drop 0.1 percent. Excluding food and energy prices, they expected a 0.1 percent increase.</p>
<p>The drop in producer prices in February was the largest since October 1994, when prices also fell by 0.4 percent. The January/February fall in producer prices was the first back-to-back drop since September/October 1994.</p>
<p>Computer prices fell by a record 5.2 percent last month, after a 0.3 percent fall in January.</p>
<p>The 5.9 percent fall in crude goods prices was the largest since an 8.1 percent decline in February 1991.</p>
